          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="footnote">
        From AMD's ROCm GPU hardware specification tables (VGPR file per WGP
        &divide; 4 SIMDs &divide; 128 bytes per wave32 VGPR). *RDNA1 value from
        the table's <code>gfx1012</code> entry, the same generation. Every RDNA
        part listed has 128 KiB of LDS per WGP; a single workgroup can use up to
        64 KiB.
      </p>

      <p>Two structural breaks worth memorizing:</p>
      <ol class="distil-ol">
        <li>
          <strong>RDNA1/2 &rarr; RDNA3</strong> is where the matrix unit
          appears. Before <code>gfx1100</code>, the fastest f16-mac path is
          <code>v_dot2_f32_f16</code> (a 2-wide packed-FMA) or
          <code>v_dot4_i32_i8</code> (dp4a). After <code>gfx1100</code>, you
          have <code>v_wmma_*</code> with a 16&times;16 accumulator per wave.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>RDNA3 &rarr; RDNA4</strong> keeps the wave32 WMMA shape and
          the 16&times;16&times;16 tile, but changes the layout: A/B packing
          halves from <code>half16_t</code> to <code>half8_t</code> per lane,
          the builtin gains a <code>_w32_gfx12</code> suffix, and the C-map
          moves. LDS stays &le;64 KB per workgroup (&le;128 KB per WGP) on
          both gens. Most kernels still need their own
          <code>.gfx12.hip</code> variant &mdash; for the incompatible
          builtin/layout, not a wider tile.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ 2 -->
    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">02</span> CDNA vs RDNA &mdash; the divergence summary</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th></th>
              <th><strong>CDNA &mdash; Instinct</strong><br/><span class="cell-sub">MFMA path, data center</span></th>
              <th><strong>RDNA &mdash; Radeon / Ryzen AI</strong><br/><span class="cell-sub">WMMA path, consumer</span></th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Wavefront</td><td><strong>64 threads</strong></td><td><strong>32 threads</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Matrix unit</td><td><strong>MFMA</strong> (<code>v_mfma_*</code>) &mdash; 16&times;16&times;16 fp16, 32&times;32&times;8 bf16, FP8 on CDNA 3</td><td><strong>WMMA</strong> (<code>v_wmma_*</code>) &mdash; 16&times;16&times;16 fp16 on RDNA3 and RDNA4; gfx12 halves A/B packing to half8 with a <code>_w32_gfx12</code> builtin</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Memory</td><td>HBM (192 GB HBM3 on MI300X; 288 GB HBM3E on MI355X)</td><td>GDDR6 (8&ndash;32 GB) or shared LPDDR5X system memory (Strix Halo)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cache</td><td>Large L2 per XCD; 256 MiB Infinity Cache on MI300X / MI355X</td><td>Small L2 + <strong>Infinity Cache</strong> (96 MiB on 7900 XTX; 64 MiB on R9700; 32 MiB on Strix Halo)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Graphics</td><td class="warn">None (no rasterizer, no RT, no display)</td><td class="ok">Full graphics + RT pipeline</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Transistor budget</td><td>More CUs, bigger matrix engines, more HBM controllers</td><td>Display engines, rasterizer, RT cores compete with compute</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Typical port</td><td><code>gfx908 / gfx90a / gfx942 / gfx950</code></td><td><code>gfx1010 &rarr; gfx1201</code></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        <strong>Why this matters for code:</strong> the same <code>.hip</code>
        file compiles for either family &mdash; but a CDNA kernel ported to
        RDNA hits three landmines: wavefront width (64 &rarr; 32 halves the
        lanes seen by <code>__shfl_*</code>), matrix intrinsic (<code>v_mfma_*</code>
        doesn&apos;t exist on RDNA &mdash; you must call <code>v_wmma_*</code>),
        and memory-tier sizing (96 MB Infinity Cache on the 7900 XTX changes your tiling
        math vs HBM-direct).
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ 3 -->
    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">03</span> WMMA &mdash; one instruction, one 16&times;16 tile</h2>
      <p>
        The WMMA family is RDNA3+'s answer to CDNA's MFMA. Same shape &mdash;
        a fused <code>D = A &times; B + C</code> over a 16&times;16 tile
        &mdash; but reachable from wave32 (half the lanes) and from a
        fundamentally consumer-silicon caches.
      </p>
      <p>The canonical RDNA3 builtin (used in hipfire's <code>gemm_f16_wmma.hip</code>):</p>
      <pre class="prose-pre"><code>{`// WMMA layout (gfx11 RDNA3): __builtin_amdgcn_wmma_f32_16x16x16_f16_w32(a, b, c)
// computes  D = A x B + C over one 16x16 tile (ISA-defined lane layout).
//
// A is half16_t (16 fp16 values per lane)
// B is half16_t
// C / D are float8_t (8 fp32 accumulators per lane on wave32)
// (gfx12 RDNA4 keeps 16x16x16 but packs A/B as half8_t per lane, uses a
//  _w32_gfx12 builtin, and remaps C — see “What gfx12 WMMA changes” below.)

typedef _Float16 __attribute__((ext_vector_type(16))) half16_t;
typedef float    __attribute__((ext_vector_type(8)))  float8_t;

__launch_bounds__(32, 2)
extern "C" __global__ void gemm_f16_wmma(
    const _Float16* __restrict__ W, const float* __restrict__ X,
    float*          __restrict__ Y, int M, int K, int N)
{
    // Grid: [ceil(M/16), ceil(N/16)],  Block: [32]
    float8_t acc = {0.f, 0.f, 0.f, 0.f, 0.f, 0.f, 0.f, 0.f};
    for (int k0 = 0; k0 < K; k0 += 16) {
        half16_t a_reg, b_reg;
        // ...load 16x16 tiles into a_reg and b_reg...
        acc = __builtin_amdgcn_wmma_f32_16x16x16_f16_w32(a_reg, b_reg, acc);
    }
    // ...store acc back to Y...
}`}</code></pre>

      <div class="distil-fig" data-widget="wmma">
        <div class="fig-caption">interactive &middot; v_wmma_f32_NxNxN_f16 &middot; one issue, one tile</div>
