    <!-- ============================================================ 5 -->
    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">05</span> Why CDNA &ldquo;throws away&rdquo; graphics and RDNA doesn't</h2>
      <p>The mirror image of the CDNA page's transistor-budget section:</p>

      <div class="budget-grid">
        <div class="budget-card budget-plus">
          <div class="budget-tag">RDNA spends transistors on  ( + )</div>
          <ul>
            <li>Rasterizers, ROPs, display engines, video encode/decode.</li>
            <li>Ray-tracing accelerators (since RDNA2).</li>
            <li>Mesh shaders, primitive shaders.</li>
            <li>Smaller, latency-optimized L0 / L1.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="budget-card budget-minus">
          <div class="budget-tag">What it gives up vs CDNA  ( &minus; )</div>
          <ul>
            <li>Fewer CUs (96 on 7900 XTX vs 304 on MI300X &mdash; about 3&times;).</li>
            <li>No HBM (24 GB GDDR6 vs 192 GB HBM3 &mdash; about 8&times;).</li>
            <li>No FP8 / FP6 / FP4 MFMA &mdash; RDNA3 WMMA is fp16 / bf16 / i8 / i4 only; RDNA4 WMMA adds FP8 mixes.</li>
            <li>No XCD-level multi-die packaging &mdash; RDNA3 chiplets are MCDs (memory) not compute.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        <strong>The result for an LLM workload:</strong> fewer CUs and much
        less memory bandwidth than an Instinct part, but the same kind of
        16&times;16 matrix tile per wave, on a card that sits in a workstation
        or a laptop-class APU. Decode is bandwidth-bound on both; what RDNA
        changes is how much bandwidth and memory you have to work with.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ 6 -->
    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">06</span> The wave32 occupancy table</h2>
      <p>
        WMMA GEMMs are register-hungry: the 8-float accumulator alone costs 8
        VGPRs per lane, and A/B fragments, rings and staging come on top. The
        count has to fit the wave32 occupancy curve &mdash; drag the slider:
      </p>
      <p class="footnote">
        VGPR-capacity model for <strong>gfx1100</strong> and
        <strong>gfx1201</strong>: 1536 VGPRs per SIMD, wave32 allocation
        granule 24, cap 16 waves/SIMD &mdash;
        <code>min(16, floor(1536 / (ceil(vgprs/24) &times; 24)))</code>.
        LDS, thread/workgroup, and barrier limits are explicitly excluded.
      </p>

      <div class="distil-fig" id="occ-widget" data-widget="occupancy">
        <div class="fig-caption">interactive &middot; wave32 occupancy explorer</div>
        <div class="occ-body">
          <div class="occ-slider-row">
            <label for="occ-input">VGPR count per lane (compiler .vgpr_count)</label>
            <input id="occ-input" type="range" min="32" max="256" step="1" value="178" aria-label="VGPR count per lane (compiler .vgpr_count)" />
            <output id="occ-val" for="occ-input">178</output>
          </div>
          <div class="occ-cards">
            <div class="occ-card">
              <div class="occ-card-head">
                <span class="occ-card-arch">gfx1100 · 7900 XTX</span>
                <span class="occ-card-meta">1536 VGPRs / SIMD · granule 24</span>
              </div>
              <div class="occ-bar-wrap">
                <div class="occ-bar" id="occ-bar-rdna3"></div>
                <div class="occ-bar-ticks">
                  <span>0</span><span>4</span><span>8</span><span>12</span><span>16</span>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="occ-result">
                <span class="occ-result-val" id="occ-val-rdna3">16</span>
                <span class="occ-result-label">waves / SIMD</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="occ-card">
              <div class="occ-card-head">
                <span class="occ-card-arch">gfx1201 · R9700</span>
                <span class="occ-card-meta">1536 VGPRs / SIMD · granule 24</span>
              </div>
              <div class="occ-bar-wrap">
                <div class="occ-bar accent" id="occ-bar-rdna4"></div>
                <div class="occ-bar-ticks">
                  <span>0</span><span>4</span><span>8</span><span>12</span><span>16</span>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="occ-result">
                <span class="occ-result-val accent" id="occ-val-rdna4">16</span>
                <span class="occ-result-label">waves / SIMD</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="occ-verdict" id="occ-verdict">
            High VGPR use (~178 VGPRs/thread, alloc 192): <strong>8/16 waves/SIMD — VGPR-capacity bound only; LDS, thread/workgroup, and barrier limits excluded.</strong>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <details class="distil-details">
        <summary>full lookup table</summary>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="num">VGPRs / thread (.vgpr_count)</th>
                <th class="num">gfx1100 waves / SIMD<br/><span class="cell-sub">1536 pool · granule 24 · cap 16</span></th>
                <th class="num">gfx1201 waves / SIMD<br/><span class="cell-sub">1536 pool · granule 24 · cap 16</span></th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="num">≤ 96</td>   <td class="num">16</td><td class="num">16</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="num">97–120</td> <td class="num">12</td><td class="num">12</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="num">121–144</td><td class="num">10</td><td class="num">10</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="num">145–168</td><td class="num">9</td> <td class="num">9</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="num">169–192</td><td class="num">8</td> <td class="num">8</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="num">193–216</td><td class="num">7</td> <td class="num">7</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="num">217–240</td><td class="num">6</td> <td class="num">6</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="num">241–256</td><td class="num">6</td> <td class="num">6</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </details>

      <p>A worked 0.4.1 example and two rules of thumb:</p>
      <ul class="distil-ul">
        <li>
          <strong>The gfx1201 HC-down tile</strong> (<code>qwen4_wmma_lds_160_80_32_80_k32_p_s4</code>,
          five 32&times;80 waves) uses <strong>178 VGPRs</strong>: by the model
          above, 8 waves per SIMD. The bigger lever was LDS: the kernel it
          replaced needed 24,576&nbsp;B per workgroup, which capped a WGP at
          five workgroups; the new tile needs 15,360&nbsp;B, so eight fit in a
          WGP's 128&nbsp;KiB. Same accumulation order, byte-identical output.
        </li>
        <li><strong>High theoretical occupancy + low VALUBusy = memory-bound.</strong> More occupancy won't help; you need more in-flight memory transactions per wave (interleave, splits, prefetch).</li>
        <li><strong><code>.private_segment_fixed_size: 0</code></strong> is the reliable &ldquo;no spills&rdquo; indicator from the AMDGPU note section &mdash; the <code>vgpr_spill_count</code> field is sometimes elided by the toolchain when zero.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Reading those numbers out of a compiled <code>.hsaco</code> (from the <code>gfx-kernel-metadata</code> skill):</p>
      <pre class="prose-pre"><code>{`ARCH=gfx1100
# 1. Unbundle the offload container into a real ELF
/opt/rocm/llvm/bin/clang-offload-bundler --type=o --unbundle \\
    --input=kernel.hsaco --output=/tmp/kernel.elf \\
    --targets=hipv4-amdgcn-amd-amdhsa--$ARCH
# 2. Read AMDGPU notes
/opt/rocm/llvm/bin/llvm-readelf --notes /tmp/kernel.elf
# → .vgpr_count, .group_segment_fixed_size, .private_segment_fixed_size,
#   .wavefront_size: 32 (RDNA) / 64 (CDNA)`}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ 7 -->
    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">07</span> How hipfire 0.4.1 uses WMMA</h2>
      <p>
        The &sect;&nbsp;04 regime split shows up directly in 0.4.1's routing:
        decode and few-row verify stay on GEMV-style kernels, and wide prefill
        chunks go to WMMA. For Flash-Next on the two release cards the line is
        drawn at <strong>512 rows</strong> per prefill chunk; the chunk itself
        is 8,192 rows on Strix Halo and 4,096 on the R9700.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Flash-Next prefill route</th><th>arch</th><th>WMMA form</th><th>admitted when</th><th>exactness</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Symmetric IU4 MoE experts</strong> with A4 activations</td>
              <td><code>gfx1151</code><br /><code>gfx1201</code></td>
              <td><code>v_wmma_i32_16x16x16_iu4</code><br /><code>v_wmma_i32_16x16x32_iu4</code></td>
              <td>chunks &ge; 512 rows, every expert header on the symmetric grid</td>
              <td>KLD-gated</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Gathered QSA attention</strong> (F16 K, block-transposed V)</td>
              <td><code>gfx1151</code>, <code>gfx1201</code></td>
              <td>F16 &rarr; F32 WMMA</td>
              <td>chunks &ge; 512 rows not taken by the dense full-window route</td>
              <td>KLD-gated</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>MQ6 trunk projections</strong> (X staged in LDS)</td>
              <td><code>gfx1201</code>, <code>gfx1151</code></td>
              <td>F16 &rarr; F32 WMMA, ascending-K chain</td>
              <td>gfx1201: chunks &ge; 512 rows; gfx1151: per-shape tile table</td>
              <td>byte-exact</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>HC GEMMs and BF16 projections</strong></td>
              <td><code>gfx1201</code></td>
              <td>gfx12 F16 WMMA</td>
              <td>prefill</td>
              <td>KLD-gated</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The IU4 route is the most RDNA-specific of these: once the weights sit
        on a symmetric 4-bit grid and the activations are quantized to 4 bits,
        an expert GEMM is an integer WMMA with one scale at the end
        (<a href="/learn/magnum-quant">MagnumQuant &sect;&nbsp;04</a>). On gfx12 the
        IU4 instruction takes K&nbsp;=&nbsp;32 per issue instead of 16. Those
        expert kernels, the gathered QSA attention and the MQ6 trunk GEMMs are
        built with <a href="/learn/peacemaker">PeaceMaker</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Outside Flash-Next, the dense HFQ4 Q/K/V GEMM on RDNA3 has one more
        WMMA arm: from 128 rows, an MMQ path quantizes activations to Q8_1 and
        runs integer WMMA, ahead of the F16 WMMA kernel (&sect;&nbsp;09).
      </p>

      <h3>What it buys you</h3>
      <div class="lx-buys">
        <div class="lx-buys-head">synthetic pp8192 prefill &middot; 0.4.1 release defaults &middot; medians of three fresh processes</div>
        <div class="lx-stats">
          <div class="lx-stat">
            <div class="lx-stat-val accent">{haloCold.pp8192.median.toLocaleString("en-US")}<span class="unit">tok/s</span></div>
            <div class="lx-stat-label">Flash-Next, Strix Halo (gfx1151)</div>
            <div class="lx-stat-sub">range {haloCold.pp8192.min.toLocaleString("en-US")}–{haloCold.pp8192.max.toLocaleString("en-US")}</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lx-stat">
            <div class="lx-stat-val accent">{r9700Fn.pp8192.median.toLocaleString("en-US")}<span class="unit">tok/s</span></div>
            <div class="lx-stat-label">Flash-Next, R9700 (gfx1201)</div>
            <div class="lx-stat-sub">range {r9700Fn.pp8192.min.toLocaleString("en-US")}–{r9700Fn.pp8192.max.toLocaleString("en-US")}</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lx-stat">
            <div class="lx-stat-val">{qwen27b.gpus.find((g) => g.arch === "gfx1201")!.pp8192.toLocaleString("en-US")}<span class="unit">tok/s</span></div>
            <div class="lx-stat-label">Qwen3.8 27B MQ4, R9700</div>
            <div class="lx-stat-sub">8K-token prompt; AR decode {qwen27b.gpus.find((g) => g.arch === "gfx1201")!.decAr} tok/s on the same GPU</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="lx-buys-foot">
          Compare decode on the same cards: Flash-Next greedy AR runs at
          {haloCold.decAr.median} tok/s on Strix Halo and {r9700Fn.decAr.median} tok/s on the R9700.
          A wide prefill chunk reuses every weight it reads across hundreds of
          rows, which is the regime where WMMA throughput, not bandwidth, sets
          the pace. The R9700 Flash-Next cells were measured on an earlier build
          of the 0.4.1 release cycle; the 27B cells are on the release build.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ 8 -->
    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">08</span> Memory hierarchy &mdash; RDNA edition</h2>
      <p>
        Same layered story as CDNA, with consumer-silicon capacities. For
        decode the tier that matters is the bottom one: every active weight is
        read once per token, so the rate at which weights can be streamed sets
        the decode ceiling.
      </p>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>tier</th><th>scope</th><th class="num">RX 7900 XTX</th><th class="num">R9700</th><th class="num">Strix Halo (8060S)</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>VGPRs</td><td>per SIMD</td><td class="num">192 KiB</td><td class="num">192 KiB</td><td class="num">192 KiB</td></tr>
            <tr><td>LDS</td><td>per WGP</td><td class="num">128 KiB</td><td class="num">128 KiB</td><td class="num">128 KiB</td></tr>
            <tr><td>L0 vector cache</td><td>per CU</td><td class="num">32 KiB</td><td class="num">32 KiB</td><td class="num">32 KiB</td></tr>
            <tr><td>L2</td><td>device</td><td class="num">6 MiB</td><td class="num">8 MiB</td><td class="num">2 MiB</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Infinity Cache</td><td>device</td><td class="num">96 MiB</td><td class="num">64 MiB</td><td class="num">32 MiB</td></tr>
            <tr><td>DRAM</td><td>device</td><td class="num">24 GB GDDR6<br /><span class="cell-sub">960 GB/s</span></td><td class="num">32 GB GDDR6<br /><span class="cell-sub">640 GB/s</span></td><td class="num">shared LPDDR5X<br /><span class="cell-sub">256 GB/s</span></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="footnote">
        Cache and register capacities from AMD's ROCm GPU specification
        tables; DRAM size and peak bandwidth from AMD's product pages.
      </p>

      <p>
        The standard rule still holds: <strong>every step down the ladder is
        much slower</strong>, so a kernel that touches the same byte twice
        should get the second touch from registers or LDS, not DRAM. That is
        what the PM MQ6 twin on the <a href="/learn/peacemaker">PeaceMaker</a>
        page does: it stages activations in LDS and keeps B fragments in an
        8-deep register ring. A large MoE like Flash-Next (a 125&nbsp;GB weights
        file) does not fit in any cache, or even in a discrete card's VRAM:
        every prefill chunk re-streams the routed experts, and on the R9700
        part of the experts live in host memory.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ 9 -->
    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">09</span> Dispatch &mdash; fast paths first, baseline last</h2>
      <p>
        <code>rdna-compute</code>'s <code>Gpu</code> methods are the
        kernel-selection hot path in hipfire: GEMM, GEMV, attention, norm and
        MoE calls route through them. The shape is always the same:
        arch-feature predicates from <code>arch_caps</code>, fast paths first,
        baseline last. Here is the real prefill (more than one row) cascade of
        the dense HFQ4 Q/K/V GEMM in 0.4.1:
      </p>

      <div class="distil-fig" data-widget="dispatch">
        <div class="fig-caption">interactive &middot; dispatch tree &middot; click an arch to see which kernel branch wins</div>
        <div class="dispatch-body">
          <div class="dispatch-archs" role="tablist" aria-label="Select GPU architecture">
            <button class="dispatch-arch" data-arch="gfx1010" role="tab" aria-selected="false">
              <span class="dispatch-arch-name">gfx1010</span>
              <span class="dispatch-arch-sub">5700 XT &middot; RDNA1</span>
            </button>
            <button class="dispatch-arch" data-arch="gfx1030" role="tab" aria-selected="false">
              <span class="dispatch-arch-name">gfx1030</span>
              <span class="dispatch-arch-sub">6950 XT &middot; RDNA2</span>
            </button>
            <button class="dispatch-arch active" data-arch="gfx1100" role="tab" aria-selected="true">
              <span class="dispatch-arch-name">gfx1100</span>
              <span class="dispatch-arch-sub">7900 XTX &middot; RDNA3</span>
            </button>
            <button class="dispatch-arch" data-arch="gfx1151" role="tab" aria-selected="false">
              <span class="dispatch-arch-name">gfx1151</span>
              <span class="dispatch-arch-sub">Strix Halo &middot; RDNA3.5</span>
            </button>
            <button class="dispatch-arch" data-arch="gfx1201" role="tab" aria-selected="false">
              <span class="dispatch-arch-name">gfx1201</span>
              <span class="dispatch-arch-sub">R9700 &middot; RDNA4</span>
            </button>
          </div>

          <div class="dispatch-tree" aria-live="polite">
            <div class="dispatch-call">gemm_qkv_hfq4g256(...)</div>
            <div class="dispatch-branch" data-branch="mmq">
              <span class="dispatch-pred">should_use_mmq(rows)</span>
              <span class="dispatch-arrow">&rarr;</span>
              <span class="dispatch-kernel">MMQ: Q8_1 activations, integer WMMA</span>
              <span class="dispatch-archs-hint">gfx11 from 128 rows</span>
            </div>
            <div class="dispatch-branch" data-branch="mmq-sdot4">
              <span class="dispatch-pred">has_hfq4_mmq</span>
              <span class="dispatch-arrow">&rarr;</span>
              <span class="dispatch-kernel">wave32 MMQ (packed int8 dot)</span>
              <span class="dispatch-archs-hint">gfx1011 / 1012 / 1030&ndash;1032</span>
            </div>
            <div class="dispatch-branch" data-branch="gfx12">
              <span class="dispatch-pred">has_wmma_w32_gfx12</span>
              <span class="dispatch-arrow">&rarr;</span>
              <span class="dispatch-kernel">F16 WMMA (gfx12 layout)</span>
              <span class="dispatch-archs-hint">gfx1200 / 1201</span>
            </div>
            <div class="dispatch-branch" data-branch="gfx11">
              <span class="dispatch-pred">has_wmma_w32</span>
              <span class="dispatch-arrow">&rarr;</span>
              <span class="dispatch-kernel">F16 WMMA (RDNA3)</span>
              <span class="dispatch-archs-hint">gfx1100&ndash;1103 / 1150&ndash;1152</span>
            </div>
            <div class="dispatch-branch" data-branch="dot2">
              <span class="dispatch-pred">has_dot2_f32_f16</span>
              <span class="dispatch-arrow">&rarr;</span>
              <span class="dispatch-kernel">dot2 path</span>
              <span class="dispatch-archs-hint">RDNA1.1 / RDNA2 when MMQ can't take the shape</span>
            </div>
            <div class="dispatch-branch" data-branch="baseline">
              <span class="dispatch-pred">(default)</span>
              <span class="dispatch-arrow">&rarr;</span>
              <span class="dispatch-kernel">packed fp16 baseline</span>
              <span class="dispatch-archs-hint">gfx1010 / 1013 (no packed DOT)</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="dispatch-verdict" id="dispatch-verdict">
            <strong>gfx1100</strong> takes the <strong>F16 WMMA (RDNA3)</strong> path below 128 rows; from 128 rows the MMQ arm (integer WMMA on Q8_1 activations) wins first.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <pre class="prose-pre"><code>{`// gemm_qkv_hfq4g256, prefill (rows > 1), trimmed from crates/rdna-compute/src/gemm.rs.
// The CDNA3 rocBLAS and gfx906 wave64 arms that come first are covered on the CDNA page.
if self.arch_caps.should_use_mmq(rows) {            // gfx11: rows >= 128 (gfx906: >= 8)
    return mmq_set_prequant(...);                   // Q8_1 activations, integer WMMA
}
if self.arch_caps.has_hfq4_mmq() && m % 128 == 0 {  // RDNA1.1 / RDNA2 (sdot4)
    return self.gemm_qkv_hfq4g256_mmq(...);
}
if self.arch_caps.has_wmma_w32_gfx12() {            // RDNA4: gfx1200 / 1201
    return self.gemm_qkv_hfq4g256_wmma_gfx12(...);
}
if self.arch_caps.has_wmma_w32() {                  // RDNA3 / 3.5
    return self.gemm_qkv_hfq4g256_wmma(...);
}
if self.arch_caps.has_dot2_f32_f16() {              // gfx1011/1012/1030-1032
    return self.gemm_qkv_hfq4g256_dot2(...);
}
self.gemm_qkv_hfq4g256_fp16(...)                    // gfx1010/1013: v_pk_fma_f16`}</code></pre>

      <p>Two rules from hipfire's architecture doc:</p>
      <ol class="distil-ol">
        <li>
          <strong>Predicates are arch-feature checks</strong>, not inline
          <code>arch.starts_with(...)</code> chains. New silicon = update one
          predicate, not 40 call sites.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No unreachable branches.</strong> When a new arch absorbs a
          check that was matched by an older
          <code>|| starts_with("gfxN")</code> clause, drop the redundant
          clause in the same diff.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>The per-arch kernel-file naming convention follows the dispatch:</p>
      <pre class="prose-pre"><code>{`kernels/src/gemm_qkv_hfq4g256_wmma.hip            # default WMMA (RDNA3)
kernels/src/gemm_qkv_hfq4g256_wmma.gfx12.hip      # gfx12xx override (gfx12 WMMA layout)
kernels/src/gemv_hfq4g256.gfx1030.v4.hip          # chip-specific versioned
kernels/src/gemv_hfq6g256_residual_wave64.hip     # wave64 (CDNA fall-back)`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Which source a module uses is decided in Rust (<code>kernels.rs</code>
        includes the <code>.gfx12.hip</code> override where one exists) and
        recorded in an exact source/flags registry;
        <code>scripts/compile-kernels.sh</code> builds the per-arch kernel packs
        from that registry, and the runtime verifies each installed object's
        SHA-256 before loading it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ 10 -->
    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">10</span> Summary &mdash; what to remember</h2>
      <ul class="distil-ul">
        <li><strong>RDNA wave width is 32</strong>, not 64. <code>__shfl_*</code>, occupancy math and the <code>_w32</code> WMMA builtins all key off this.</li>
        <li><strong>WMMA is the RDNA3+ matrix unit</strong>: <code>v_wmma_*</code> over 16&times;16&times;16 tiles on RDNA3 / 3.5 (half16 A/B) and RDNA4 (half8 gfx12 layout, plus FP8 inputs and a K&nbsp;=&nbsp;32 IU4 form). <code>gfx1010</code> has no packed DOT and no matrix unit; RDNA1.1 / RDNA2 add <code>v_dot4_i32_i8</code> / <code>v_dot2_f32_f16</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>0.4.1 draws the WMMA line at 512-row prefill chunks</strong> for Flash-Next: IU4 experts with A4 activations, gathered F16 QSA attention, MQ6 trunk projections and gfx1201 HC GEMMs. Decode and verify stay on GEMV-style kernels.</li>
        <li><strong>Dispatch is feature-gated</strong> (<code>should_use_mmq</code>, <code>has_hfq4_mmq</code>, <code>has_wmma_w32_gfx12</code>, <code>has_wmma_w32</code>, <code>has_dot2_f32_f16</code> in <code>arch_caps.rs</code>), not arch-string-matched.</li>
        <li><strong>WMMA kernels are register- and LDS-hungry.</strong> gfx1100, gfx1151 and gfx1201 all have 1536 VGPRs per SIMD; the 0.4.1 gfx1201 HC-down tile (178 VGPRs, 15,360&nbsp;B LDS) is a worked example of trading tile shape for occupancy.</li>
        <li><strong>Infinity Cache</strong> sits between L2 and DRAM: 96&nbsp;MiB on the 7900 XTX, 64&nbsp;MiB on the R9700, 32&nbsp;MiB on Strix Halo.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ 11 -->
    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">11</span> Primary sources</h2>
      <ul class="distil-ul">
        <li>AMD GPUOpen &mdash; <a href="https://gpuopen.com/learn/wmma_on_rdna3/">WMMA on RDNA3</a>: 16&times;16-only tiles, <code>…_16x16x16_…</code> intrinsics, 512 FLOPS/clk/CU fp16 dense.</li>
        <li>AMD GPUOpen &mdash; <a href="https://gpuopen.com/learn/using_matrix_core_amd_rdna4/">Using matrix core on RDNA4</a>: 16&times;16 WMMA with gfx12 packing and <code>_w32_gfx12</code> builtins.</li>
        <li>AMD GPUOpen &mdash; <a href="https://gpuopen.com/learn/occupancy-explained/">Occupancy explained</a>: 7900 XTX VGPR pool, wave slots, allocation granularity.</li>
        <li>AMD &mdash; <a href="https://rocm.docs.amd.com/en/latest/reference/gpu-arch-specs.html">ROCm GPU hardware specifications</a>: CU counts, LDS, L2, Infinity Cache and VGPR file sizes in &sect;&nbsp;01 and &sect;&nbsp;08.</li>
        <li>AMD &mdash; <a href="https://www.amd.com/en/products/graphics/desktops/radeon/7000-series/amd-radeon-rx-7900xtx.html">Radeon RX 7900 XTX</a>, <a href="https://www.amd.com/en/products/graphics/workstations/radeon-ai-pro/ai-9000-series/amd-radeon-ai-pro-r9700.html">Radeon AI PRO R9700</a>, <a href="https://www.amd.com/en/products/processors/laptop/ryzen/ai-300-series/amd-ryzen-ai-max-plus-395.html">Ryzen AI Max+ 395</a>: memory size and bandwidth.</li>
        <li>hipfire v0.4.1 &mdash; <a href={tagBlob("crates/rdna-compute/src/arch_caps.rs")}><code>crates/rdna-compute/src/arch_caps.rs</code></a> and <a href={tagBlob("crates/rdna-compute/src/gemm.rs")}><code>crates/rdna-compute/src/gemm.rs</code></a>: predicates and the dispatch cascade.</li>
        <li>hipfire v0.4.1 &mdash; <a href={RELEASE_CHANGELOG}>CHANGELOG</a> (Flash-Next WMMA routes, the HC-down tile) and the <a href={RELEASE_URL}>release notes</a> (all measured figures on this page).</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

